>By default, every table contains a Primary Name column that is used by lookup columns when establishing relationships with other tables.
>In Dataverse attachments can be enabled within a table, Attachments are enabled for standard tables.
>Choice columns are also known as picklists and option sets.
>Choice columns are key/value pairs contain items with an integer value and a label.
>Local choices can only be used by the table and column that they're created against and can't be reused on other tables. 
This approach is only recommended for advanced users that have a specific need for a local choice.
You can configure a choice column to allow selection of multiple options. This setting can't be changed after the column has been created


>Rollup - A read-only column that is calculated by aggregating values from table rows and columns in a one-to-many relationship.
    Rollup columns:
.         Max of 10 for each table
·         Max of 100 for each organization
·         Can't trigger workflows or flows

>While working with Dataverse, keep this limitation in mind.There is not limit of number of columns, but more than 100 columns mean you 
need to rethink you table.There is upper limit of amount of data stored in single record.
Dataverse utilizes Azure SQL as the data store behind the scenes inherits some SQL Server behaviors (like row structure, indexing, column types).
Dataverse does NOT enforce a fixed maximum size for a single record,
    Small fields (text, numbers, lookups) are stored in the SQL row
    Large fields (long text, file, image) are stored outside the row
    This means a single record can grow very large because off-row storage doesn’t count toward SQL’s 8 KB row limit.
Dataverse has three storage buckets:
    Database capacity
    File capacity
    Log capacity
As long as you have available storage, a record can keep growing.
